{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abbc945fc7a819a5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8007\n3\ttitle: '[TP] Feature Store Admin UI — Self-Service Instance Deployment and Management'\n4\tstatus: In Progress\n5\tpriority: Major\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- dashboard-tangerine-scrum\n8\t- dashboard-tangerine-supported\n9\t- feature-store\n10\t- needs-uxd\n11\t- refined\n12\tlinks:\n13\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7342'\n14\t- 'related-strategy: RHAISTRAT-7215'\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Problem Statement\n18\t\n19\tPlatform administrators who deploy and configure Red Hat OpenShift AI Feature Store have no UI surface in the RHOAI dashboard to do so. Today, setting up a Feature Store instance requires hand-authoring Kubernetes CRD YAML with no guidance, validating the deployment by navigating multiple OpenShift pages, and managing the lifecycle entirely from the command line.\n20\t\n21\tThis creates significant friction:\n22\t\n23\t* Admins must understand complex CRD structure (registry type, store backends, authorization, scaling) before they can deploy anything\n24\t* There is no way to track deployment progress or view instance status from within the RHOAI dashboard\n25\t* There is no way to list, inspect, or remove Feature Store instances from the dashboard\n26\t\n27\t## Desired Outcome\n28\t\n29\tA platform administrator can deploy, configure, monitor, and manage Feature Store instances entirely from within the RHOAI dashboard — with no YAML hand-editing or CLI access required for standard workflows.\n30\t\n31\t## Acceptance Criteria\n32\t\n33\t* A platform admin can deploy a new Feature Store instance from the RHOAI dashboard by filling out a guided form — no YAML editing or CLI access required\n34\t* A platform admin can view all Feature Store instances in their cluster, including deployment status, from a dashboard management page\n35\t* A platform admin can inspect the configuration of any Feature Store instance and delete it from the dashboard\n36\t* A platform admin can track deployment progress (including readiness status) from within the dashboard after creating an instance\n37\t* Non-admin users (viewers) can browse Feature Store instances but cannot create or delete them\n38\t* Disconnected / air-gapped deployments are considered in scope — the admin UI must function without external network access\n39\t\n40\t## What This Is NOT\n41\t\n42\t* **Not a data scientist or non-admin UI** — instance creation and management is admin-only; non-admin Feature Store access (read, connect from workbenches) is covered separately\n43\t* **Not Feature Store workbench/notebook integration** — that is RHAISTRAT-7215 (separate track)\n44\t* **Not Feature Store quality monitoring or drift dashboards** — separate 3.5 item\n45\t* **Not multi-tenant or multi-project namespace management** — deferred to a follow-on feature once the multi-project story is resolved\n46\t* **Not a permissions management UI** — authorization policy configuration (permissions.py replacement) may follow as a Phase 2\n47\t\n48\t## Business Justification\n49\t\n50\tFeature Store has data scientist-facing UI today (read-only browsing, workbench connection), but the admin setup path is entirely CLI/YAML. This asymmetry makes Feature Store inaccessible to the non-engineering administrators who typically own RHOAI cluster configuration in enterprise accounts.\n51\t\n52\tThe admin UI gap is not theoretical — it has been raised in user testing sessions and confirmed by the AI BU. Addressing it in 3.5 TP reduces the support burden for Feature Store deployments.\n53\t\n54\tA working prototype has been validated on a real cluster by the Feature Store engineering team, confirming this is deliverable and the scope is well-understood.\n55\t\n56\t## Reference Documents\n57\t\n58\t* [#wg-feast-ui Slack thread — Mar 30, 2026]([internal link removed]) — Live prototype demo, team validation, formal-path decision\n59\t* RHAISTRAT-7215 — Feature Store & Workbenches Integration Phase 2 (separate track — do not conflate)\n60\t* Engineering prototype: [odh-dashboard draft PR #7013](https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard/pull/7013)\n61\t\n62\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n63\t\n64\t### TL;DR\n65\t\n66\tAdd a Feature Store admin management surface to the RHOAI dashboard so platform administrators can deploy, monitor, inspect, and delete FeatureStore instances through a guided form UI instead of hand-authoring CRD YAML. The implementation extends the existing odh-dashboard Feast integration (which already has `feast.dev/featurestores` RBAC and `/api/featurestores` proxy routes) with new admin pages for CRUD operations against the `FeatureStore` CRD (feast.dev/v1), enforcing admin-only write access via existing kube-rbac-proxy and K8s RBAC patterns.\n67\t\n68\t**Components**: odh-dashboard, feast-operator\n69\t\n70\t### Technical Approach\n71\t\n72\tThis strategy delivers a dashboard-native admin UI for Feature Store instance lifecycle management. The primary persona is the **platform administrator** who today must hand-author `FeatureStore` CRD YAML to deploy Feast instances. The target outcome is that this admin can create, monitor, inspect, and delete Feature Store instances entirely from the RHOAI dashboard.\n73\t\n74\t**Architectural Fit within odh-dashboard**\n75\t\n76\tThe odh-dashboard already integrates with the Feast operator. Specifically:\n77\t\n78\t* The dashboard's ClusterRole includes `feast.dev/featurestores` with `get, list, watch` verbs (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, RBAC section).\n79\t* The backend exposes `/api/featurestores` as a proxy route to the Feast Registry service (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, API Routes).\n80\t* The dashboard's `OdhDashboardConfig` feature flag system can gate the admin UI behind a `featureStore` or similar flag.\n81\t\n82\tTo support admin operations (create, update, delete), the implementation must:\n83\t\n84\t1. **Extend RBAC permissions**: Add `create, update, patch, delete` verbs for `feast.dev/featurestores` to the `odh-dashboard` ClusterRole. The existing `featurestore-editor-role` ClusterRole defined by the feast-operator (architecture context: `feast.md`, RBAC section) already grants these verbs and can serve as a reference for the minimum required permission set.\n85\t\n86\t1. **Add K8s API pass-through routes**: The dashboard's existing `/api/k8s/*` pass-through proxy (which forwards the authenticated user's token to the K8s API) is the primary mechanism for CRUD operations. The frontend creates/updates/deletes `FeatureStore` CRs by calling the K8s API through this proxy, meaning RBAC enforcement happens at the K8s API level using the user's own token. No new backend routes are needed for CRUD — only the existing `/api/featurestores` read proxy for Feast Registry data (instance metadata, status).\n87\t\n88\t1. **Implement guided creation form**: Build a React form wizard (PatternFly 6 components) that maps to the `FeatureStore` CRD spec (feast.dev/v1). The CRD defines configuration for: registry type, online store backend (Redis, PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, SQLite), offline store backend, authorization mode (OIDC or Kubernetes RBAC), TLS settings, scaling configuration (replicas, HPA), and materialization CronJob scheduling (architecture context: `feast.md`, CRDs section). The form must validate inputs against CRD schema constraints before submission. For disconnected/air-gapped environments, the form must not fetch external resources — all form options and validation rules must be bundled in the dashboard's static assets.\n89\t\n90\t1. **Implement management list page**: A dashboard page listing all `FeatureStore` CRs across namespaces visible to the admin. Each row shows: name, namespace, deployment status (from `.status.conditions`), creation timestamp, and actions (inspect, delete). Status polling uses the dashboard's existing K8s watch/list patterns to reflect real-time readiness state without manual refresh.\n91\t\n92\t1. **Implement instance detail/inspect view**: A read-only detail page showing the full `FeatureStore` CR spec and status. This includes: configured backends (online/offline store type, registry type), authorization mode, scaling settings, TLS status, materialization schedule, and deployment conditions. The detail page also links to the Feast UI Route (if available) for browsing feature metadata.\n93\t\n94\t1. **Implement delete flow**: Admin-initiated delete with a confirmation dialog. The dashboard sends a `DELETE` request to the K8s API via `/api/k8s/*` for the `FeatureStore` CR. The feast-operator handles resource cleanup via finalizers (architecture context: `feast.md`, the operator manages Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, CronJobs, HPAs, PDBs, RBAC resources, and Routes as owned resources).\n95\t\n96\t**RBAC and Authorization Model**\n97\t\n98\tThe admin UI enforces two authorization tiers, consistent with the RFE acceptance criteria:\n99\t\n100\t* **Admin users**: Full CRUD access to `FeatureStore` CRs. Enforced by the dashboard's `secureAdminRoute` middleware (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, Authentication & Authorization) which verifies the user is a platform admin before allowing write operations. The K8s API additionally enforces RBAC via the user's forwarded token.\n101\t* **Viewer (non-admin) users**: Read-only access. The existing `featurestore-viewer-role` ClusterRole from the feast-operator grants `get, list, watch` on `featurestores` and `featurestores/status`. Non-admin users see the list and detail pages but cannot access create/delete actions. The frontend hides write controls based on the admin status check already implemented in the dashboard.\n102\t\n103\t**Disconnected/Air-Gapped Support**\n104\t\n105\tThe admin UI must function without external network access (RFE acceptance criterion). This is achievable because:\n106\t\n107\t* The dashboard runs within the cluster and communicates with the K8s API server over internal networking.\n108\t* All UI assets (PatternFly components, form definitions, validation logic) are bundled at build time in the dashboard container image.\n109\t* The Feast operator uses `RELATED_IMAGE_*` environment variables for image references, supporting OLM image mirroring in disconnected environments (architecture context: `feast.md`, Parameterization).\n110\t* The form's store backend options (Redis, PostgreSQL, etc.) are static configuration choices that do not require network calls to enumerate.\n111\t\n112\t**Prototype Validation**\n113\t\n114\tThe RFE references a working prototype ([odh-dashboard draft PR #7013](https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard/pull/7013)) that has been validated on a real cluster by the Feature Store engineering team. This prototype confirms the feasibility of the approach and provides implementation guidance for the formal path.\n115\t\n116\t### Affected Components\n117\t\n118\t| Component | Change | Owner Team |\n119\t| --- | --- | --- |\n120\t| odh-dashboard (frontend) | New React pages: Feature Store list, create wizard, detail/inspect view, delete confirmation dialog. PatternFly 6 form components mapping to FeatureStore CRD spec. Feature flag gating in OdhDashboardConfig. | Dashboard/Frontend Engineering |\n121\t| odh-dashboard (backend) | Extend ClusterRole to add `create, update, patch, delete` verbs for `feast.dev/featurestores`. No new backend routes needed — CRUD uses existing `/api/k8s/*` pass-through, reads use existing `/api/featurestores` proxy. | Dashboard/Frontend Engineering |\n122\t| odh-dashboard (kustomize manifests) | Update `manifests/core-bases/base/sa-rbac/cluster-role.yaml` with expanded Feast RBAC. Potentially add feature flag default in OdhDashboardConfig. | Dashboard/Frontend Engineering |\n123\t| feast-operator | No code changes expected. The existing FeatureStore CRD (feast.dev/v1), RBAC roles (featurestore-editor-role, featurestore-viewer-role), and operator reconciliation logic support all required operations. May need validation that CRD webhook validation is sufficient for UI-submitted resources. | Feature Store Engineering |\n124\t\n125\t### Impacted Teams\n126\t\n127\t| Team | Components Owned | Involvement |\n128\t| --- | --- | --- |\n129\t| Dashboard/Frontend Engineering | odh-dashboard | Primary implementers — build the admin UI pages, form wizard, RBAC extension, and feature flag integration. |\n130\t| Feature Store Engineering | feast-operator, feature-server | Consultants — validate CRD schema coverage for the form wizard, confirm operator reconciliation handles dashboard-created CRs correctly, verify prototype alignment. |\n131\t| UXD Team | Design assets | Designers — create wireframes and interaction patterns for the create wizard, list page, and detail view. The `needs-uxd` label is already present on this RFE. |\n132\t| QE Team | Test suites | Testers — functional testing of CRUD flows, RBAC enforcement (admin vs. viewer), disconnected environment validation, and status tracking accuracy. |\n133\t| Documentation Team | Admin guides | Authors — document the Feature Store admin UI workflow in the RHOAI admin guide, including disconnected deployment instructions. |\n134\t\n135\t### High Level Requirements\n136\t\n137\t* **\\[P0\\]** A platform admin can deploy a new FeatureStore instance from the dashboard via a guided form wizard that covers: namespace selection, registry type, online store backend, offline store backend, authorization mode, and scaling parameters.\n138\t* **\\[P0\\]** A platform admin can view all FeatureStore instances across accessible namespaces in a dashboard management page, showing name, namespace, status, and creation time.\n139\t* **\\[P0\\]** A platform admin can inspect the configuration of any FeatureStore instance in a read-only detail view.\n140\t* **\\[P0\\]** A platform admin can delete a FeatureStore instance from the dashboard with a confirmation dialog.\n141\t* **\\[P0\\]** Deployment progress (readiness status from `.status.conditions`) is visible in the dashboard after instance creation, updating in real time.\n142\t* **\\[P1\\]** Non-admin users (viewers) can browse FeatureStore instances (list and detail views) but cannot access create or delete actions.\n143\t* **\\[P1\\]** The admin UI functions in disconnected/air-gapped environments without external network access.\n144\t* **\\[P2\\]** Form validation provides inline guidance for CRD schema constraints (e.g., required fields, valid backend combinations, scaling prerequisites).\n145\t\n146\tNote: All P0 items are directly from the RFE acceptance criteria. P1 items are also from the RFE but are less likely to block a TP ship if partially delivered. P2 represents UX polish.\n147\t\n148\t### Dependencies\n149\t\n150\t| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n151\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n152\t| FeatureStore CRD (feast.dev/v1) | Internal | Exists (Feast 0.63.0, shipped in RHOAI 3.5-ea.1) | Cannot build form wizard without stable CRD schema |\n153\t| feast-operator reconciliation | Internal | Exists | Operator must correctly reconcile FeatureStore CRs created via K8s API (not just kubectl). Already the case — the operator watches the CRD, not the creation method. |\n154\t| odh-dashboard `/api/k8s/*` pass-through | Internal | Exists | CRUD operations depend on this existing proxy pattern |\n155\t| odh-dashboard `/api/featurestores` proxy | Internal | Exists | Read operations for Feast Registry metadata use this existing route |\n156\t| `featurestore-viewer-role` and `featurestore-editor-role` ClusterRoles | Internal | Exists (defined by feast-operator) | Viewer/editor RBAC tiers depend on these existing roles |\n157\t| PatternFly 6 form components | External | Exists (v6.4.x in odh-dashboard) | Form wizard UI framework |\n158\t| UXD design review | Internal | Needed | `needs-uxd` label present — form wizard and list page designs required before implementation |\n159\t| Prototype PR #7013 | Internal | Exists (draft) | Reference implementation — not a hard dependency, but informs the formal implementation |\n160\t\n161\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n162\t\n163\t* **Security**: Admin write operations (create, update, delete FeatureStore CRs) are enforced by K8s RBAC via the user's forwarded bearer token through kube-rbac-proxy (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, Authentication & Authorization). The dashboard's `secureAdminRoute` middleware provides an additional check that the user is a platform admin. Non-admin users are restricted to read-only access via the existing `featurestore-viewer-role` ClusterRole (architecture context: `feast.md`, RBAC section).\n164\t* **Security**: All dashboard-to-K8s-API communication uses TLS 1.2+ via service-ca certificates (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, Network Architecture). No new external network connections are introduced.\n165\t* **Security**: FIPS compliance is maintained — the dashboard frontend runs on Node.js 22 (UBI 9 base with OpenSSL), and no new Go BFF module is introduced for this feature. RBAC changes are manifest-only. (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, FIPS Compliance).\n166\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: The admin UI is gated behind an `OdhDashboardConfig` feature flag, allowing progressive rollout. Clusters without the Feast component enabled see no UI changes. No changes to existing Feast CRD schema or operator behavior.\n167\t* **Availability**: The odh-dashboard runs 2 replicas behind a Service with stateless design (architecture context: `PLATFORM.md`, High Availability). Adding new UI pages does not change the availability posture.\n168\t\n169\t### Out-of-Scope\n170\t\n171\t* **Data scientist / non-admin Feature Store UI** — This strategy covers admin instance lifecycle only. Non-admin Feature Store access (browsing features, connecting from workbenches) is covered by RHAISTRAT-7215 and the existing feast-ui component.\n172\t* **Feature Store workbench/notebook integration** — Covered by RHAISTRAT-7215 (separate track). The notebook config injection via `opendatahub.io/feast-integration` label is a separate feature.\n173\t* **Feature Store quality monitoring or drift dashboards** — Separate 3.5 item.\n174\t* **Multi-tenant / multi-project namespace management** — Deferred to a follow-on feature once the multi-project story is resolved.\n175\t* **Permissions management UI** — Authorization policy configuration (Feast `permissions.py` replacement) is explicitly deferred to a potential Phase 2.\n176\t* **New BFF sidecar for Feature Store** — The implementation uses existing dashboard patterns (`/api/k8s/*` pass-through, `/api/featurestores` proxy) rather than adding a new federated BFF module. A dedicated BFF may be considered in the future if the Feature Store UI grows significantly.\n177\t* **Editing an existing FeatureStore instance** — The initial TP scope covers create, inspect, and delete. In-place editing of a deployed FeatureStore CR may be added in a follow-on iteration depending on CRD mutability constraints.\n178\t* **Feast UI embedding** — The existing feast-ui (React frontend on port 8888) is deployed by the feast-operator for browsing feature metadata. This strategy does not embed or replace it — the admin detail page links to it.\n179\t\n180\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation)\n181\t\n182\t1. Given a platform administrator is logged into the RHOAI dashboard,\n183\t\n184\t   when they navigate to the Feature Store management page and click \"Create Feature Store\",    then a guided form wizard is displayed with fields for namespace, registry type, online store backend, offline store backend, authorization mode, and scaling configuration,    measured by the form rendering all required CRD fields without error and submitting a valid `FeatureStore` CR to the K8s API.\n185\t\n186\t1. Given a platform administrator has submitted a new FeatureStore instance via the form wizard,\n187\t\n188\t   when the feast-operator begins reconciling the CR,    then the dashboard's management page shows the instance with a \"Deploying\" or equivalent status that updates to \"Ready\" when all conditions are met,    measured by the status column reflecting the `.status.conditions` of the FeatureStore CR within the dashboard's watch/poll cycle.\n189\t\n190\t1. Given a platform administrator is viewing the Feature Store management page,\n191\t\n192\t   when FeatureStore instances exist in namespaces they have access to,    then all instances are listed with name, namespace, status, and creation timestamp,    measured by the list matching the output of `kubectl get featurestores -A` for the admin's accessible namespaces.\n193\t\n194\t1. Given a platform administrator clicks on a FeatureStore instance in the list,\n195\t\n196\t   when the detail/inspect view loads,    then it displays the full CRD spec (backends, auth mode, scaling, TLS, materialization schedule) and current status conditions in a read-only format,    measured by all spec fields from the `FeatureStore` CR being rendered without truncation.\n197\t\n198\t1. Given a platform administrator clicks \"Delete\" on a FeatureStore instance,\n199\t\n200\t   when they confirm the deletion in the confirmation dialog,    then the `FeatureStore` CR is deleted via the K8s API and the feast-operator cleans up all owned resources (Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, CronJobs, HPAs, Routes),    measured by the instance disappearing from the list and `kubectl get featurestore <name> -n <ns>` returning NotFound.\n201\t\n202\t1. Given a non-admin (viewer) user is logged into the RHOAI dashboard,\n203\t\n204\t   when they navigate to the Feature Store management page,    then they can see the list of FeatureStore instances and view instance details, but the \"Create\" button and \"Delete\" action are not displayed,    measured by the UI rendering list/detail views without write controls for non-admin users.\n205\t\n206\t1. The admin UI functions in a disconnected/air-gapped RHOAI deployment — no form options, validation rules, or UI assets require external network access. Measured by deploying a FeatureStore instance from the dashboard on an air-gapped cluster with no external connectivity.\n207\t\n208\t### Effort Estimate\n209\t\n210\t**M (Medium, 3-5 sprints)**\n211\t\n212\tJustification:\n213\t\n214\t* **Component count**: Primarily a single-component change (odh-dashboard), with no code changes expected in the feast-operator. The dashboard changes span frontend (React pages, form wizard, list/detail views), backend (RBAC manifest update), and manifests (feature flag).\n215\t* **Cross-team coordination**: Moderate — requires UXD design input (wireframes for the form wizard and management page), Feature Store team consultation (CRD schema validation, prototype alignment), and QE validation (including air-gapped testing). No new API contracts or operator changes needed.\n216\t* **Technical complexity**: Low-to-moderate — the implementation follows established dashboard patterns (K8s API pass-through for CRUD, PatternFly form components, admin/viewer RBAC gating). A validated prototype already exists. The main complexity is in mapping the full `FeatureStore` CRD spec to a user-friendly form wizard with appropriate validation and defaults.\n217\t* **TP scope**: As a Technology Preview feature, the UI does not require GA-level polish, allowing iterative refinement based on TP feedback.\n218\t\n219\t### Risks\n220\t\n221\t| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\n222\t| --- | --- | --- |\n223\t| UXD design review delays | Form wizard and management page cannot be finalized without UXD input; `needs-uxd` label already present indicating this is queued but not yet started. | Engage UXD team immediately; use the existing prototype (PR #7013) as a starting design reference to unblock frontend development while formal designs are created. |\n224\t| FeatureStore CRD schema changes between Feast versions | Form wizard field mapping could break if the CRD spec changes in a Feast version bump during the 3.5 cycle. | Pin to the FeatureStore v1 API (feast.dev/v1) which is the stable version. The v1alpha1 is deprecated. Validate form fields against the CRD OpenAPI schema at build time. |\n225\t| Air-gapped form validation completeness | Form validation rules may not fully cover all CRD schema constraints, leading to invalid CRs that the operator rejects post-submission. | Derive form validation rules directly from the CRD OpenAPI schema. Implement server-side validation feedback — if the K8s API rejects the CR, surface the validation error in the UI. |\n226\t| Dashboard pod resource growth | Adding more React pages and assets increases the dashboard container's memory and startup time (already a 9+ container pod). | The Feature Store admin UI adds frontend pages only — no new BFF sidecar container. Impact on pod resources is minimal compared to adding a new BFF module. Monitor dashboard pod resource consumption in QE environments. |\n227\t| Viewer RBAC enforcement gap | Non-admin users might bypass frontend UI controls via direct K8s API access if ClusterRole grants broader permissions than intended. | RBAC enforcement is at the K8s API level via the user's own token, not just UI-level hiding. Non-admin users without `featurestore-editor-role` bindings cannot write FeatureStore CRs regardless of UI state. |\n228\t\n229\t### Assumptions\n230\t\n231\t* The existing `FeatureStore` CRD (feast.dev/v1) is stable and will not undergo breaking schema changes during the RHOAI 3.5 cycle. — **needs validation** (confirm with Feature Store engineering)\n232\t* The feast-operator correctly reconciles `FeatureStore` CRs regardless of creation method (dashboard UI vs. kubectl vs. GitOps). — **confirmed** (operator watches the CRD, not the creation method)\n233\t* The dashboard's existing `/api/k8s/*` pass-through proxy supports `FeatureStore` CR operations without additional backend code. — **confirmed** (the proxy is generic for any K8s resource)\n234\t* Platform administrators have sufficient K8s RBAC permissions to create `FeatureStore` CRs in target namespaces. — **needs validation** (confirm that admin role bindings include `feast.dev/featurestores` write verbs, or that the RBAC extension in this strategy covers it)\n235\t* The existing prototype (PR #7013) is architecturally aligned with the formal implementation path and can be used as a reference. — **needs validation** (confirm with Feature Store and Dashboard engineering leads)\n236\t* UXD design review can be completed within the first sprint to unblock frontend development. — **needs validation** (depends on UXD team capacity)\n237\t\n238\t### Open Questions\n239\t\n240\t| Question | Why It Matters | Owner |\n241\t| --- | --- | --- |\n242\t| What is the target UXD timeline for Feature Store admin UI wireframes? | Frontend development is blocked until form wizard and management page designs are finalized. The `needs-uxd` label is present but no design sprint is scheduled. | UXD Team |\n243\t| Should the create form expose all `FeatureStore` CRD fields or a curated subset for TP? | The full CRD spec is complex (registry type, online/offline store backends, authorization, TLS, scaling, materialization). Exposing all fields in TP may overwhelm admins; a curated subset may be insufficient for production deployments. | PM (Desmond Ferraby) + Feature Store Engineering |\n244\t| Does the existing prototype (PR #7013) implement the approach described in this strategy, or does it diverge? | Prototype alignment determines how much implementation can be reused vs. built from scratch. | Dashboard Engineering + Feature Store Engineering |\n245\t| Should the admin UI support editing a deployed FeatureStore instance (mutable fields), or is create+delete sufficient for TP? | If FeatureStore CRD fields are immutable after creation, edit capability is impossible. If mutable, omitting edit capability reduces TP scope but limits admin workflows. | Feature Store Engineering |\n246\t| Are there FeatureStore backend combinations that should be restricted in the form (e.g., SQLite is development-only and not suitable for production)? | Form validation should guide admins toward production-ready configurations. Without guidance, admins may deploy unsupported combinations. | Feature Store Engineering |\n247\t| No performance targets for the admin UI exist in the RFE or architecture context — are any needed for TP? | Typical admin CRUD operations have low-frequency access patterns. If performance targets are needed (e.g., list page load time with 100+ instances), they should be specified. | PM + Dashboard Engineering |\n248\t\n249\t### Supporting Documentation\n250\t\n251\t* Source RFE: [RHAIRFE-7342]([internal link removed])\n252\t* Related strategy: [RHAISTRAT-7215]([internal link removed]) — Feature Store & Workbenches Integration Phase 2 (separate track)\n253\t* Engineering prototype: [odh-dashboard draft PR #7013](https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard/pull/7013)\n254\t* Architecture context: `feast.md` (Feast component architecture, CRD spec, RBAC roles, operator reconciliation)\n255\t* Architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md` (Dashboard architecture, module federation, RBAC, API routes, authentication patterns)\n256\t* Architecture context: `PLATFORM.md` (Platform deployment architecture, disconnected support, security posture)\n257\t* Design doc: \\_To be created (pending UXD review)\\_\n258\t* ADR: \\_Not expected — this feature follows established dashboard patterns\\_\n259\t\n260\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n261\t\n262\t| Prerequisite | Status | Notes |\n263\t| --- | --- | --- |\n264\t| Architecture Review | \\_Pending human review\\_ | Low architectural risk — uses existing dashboard patterns and K8s API pass-through. No new components or integration points. |\n265\t| ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding | \\_Not applicable\\_ | No new components — changes are within the existing odh-dashboard build pipeline. |\n266\t| Licence Validation | \\_Not applicable\\_ | No new dependencies introduced. |\n267\t| Accelerator/Package Support | \\_Not applicable\\_ | No GPU or accelerator involvement. |\n268\t| Documentation Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ | Admin guide documentation needed for Feature Store deployment via dashboard. |\n269\t| UXD Support | \\_Pending human review\\_ | `needs-uxd` label present. Form wizard and management page designs required before implementation. |\n270\t| Performance Team Support | \\_Not applicable\\_ | Admin CRUD UI with low-frequency access patterns — no performance testing expected for TP. |\n271\t\n272\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n273\t\n274\t_Add technical corrections, architectural direction, component preferences, or domain expertise below. Write in declarative, cumulative form — statements that remain valid across refinement iterations. This input takes priority over architecture context when they conflict. After review: address findings, then remove the needs-attention label from Jira._\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98b7402301a08c80","agent_id":"a1cc435330cc27217","session_id":"ca7cb4a8-97e2-442a-8df6-df0a1f4664f3","uuid":"e9f56c28-608d-4a6f-bedc-4646ae4b816b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:09:39.030Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-abbc945fc7a819a5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8007"}
