{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95f3da54f8cdeb2b","type":"tool_result","content":"# Component: odh-dashboard\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/odh-dashboard.git\n- **Version**: 04cfb8c815012a4a373ebe201dccc3ea83821c31\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go, TypeScript\n- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller + Sidecar utilities\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: odh-dashboard provides the web-based management console for Red Hat OpenShift AI, combining a React/PatternFly frontend with a Go operator and ten federated Go BFF sidecars to present a unified UI for managing notebooks, model serving, model registry, experiment tracking, and AI agent operations. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:18]\n\n**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nThe odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n\nThe dashboard-operator reconciles Dashboard custom resources created by the rhods-operator, deploying and configuring the console pod alongside its BFF sidecars. The operator implements a declarative ownership model where the parent Dashboard CR controls the lifecycle of Deployments, Services, Routes, NetworkPolicies, PodDisruptionBudgets, and RBAC resources via controller-runtime's `Owns()` watches. Controller watches on platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) enable the dashboard to reflect the real-time state of the AI platform without polling. The operator uses `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\n\nAuthentication follows a two-layer model: kube-rbac-proxy fronts the Node.js backend (port 8443) performing OpenShift OAuth validation and injecting identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`), while each Go BFF sidecar independently validates Bearer tokens and performs SubjectAccessReviews against the Kubernetes API for fine-grained authorization. The agent-ops BFF extends this with per-agent RBAC via SubjectAccessReviews for agent-specific access control. The Node.js backend additionally proxies Kubernetes API calls (`/api/k8s/*`) by converting user tokens to impersonation headers, allowing the frontend to interact with the cluster API through the dashboard service boundary. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, packages/agent-ops/bff/internal/api/middleware.go:1]\n\nThe component packages a Workspace controller (reconciling kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks) alongside the dashboard operator. Workspaces are managed via StatefulSets with associated Services and PodDisruptionBudgets. Cert-manager provisions TLS certificates for the webhook and metrics endpoints. All Go binaries (operator and BFF sidecars) are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptographic operations through system OpenSSL. [source: Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18-20, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86, packages/notebooks/upstream/workspaces/controller/internal/webhook/workspace_webhook.go:44]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/odh-dashboard | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/odh-dashboard | auto_merge | main | `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, `release-auto-merge.yml` | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n----\n# Component: feast\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/feast.git\n- **Version**: 4507893b047ab297e3912147760b14cf2d4a942f\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go, Python\n- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller + Python SDK\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: Feast is a feature store platform providing a Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of online/offline feature serving, registry, and UI deployments, alongside a Python SDK and Go feature server for low-latency feature retrieval. [source: infra/feast-operator/api/v1alpha1/featurestore_types.go:706, go/internal/feast/server/http_server.go:388, sdk/python/feast/feature_server.py:412]\n\n**Detailed**: Feast consists of two primary runtime components: a Go-based Kubernetes operator (`feast-operator`) and a multi-language feature serving stack. The operator manages `FeatureStore` custom resources (API group `feast.dev`, versions `v1` and `v1alpha1`) and reconciles them into Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, RBAC resources, HorizontalPodAutoscalers, PodDisruptionBudgets, CronJobs, and OpenShift Routes. The `FeatureStoreReconciler` watches the FeatureStore CR and owns 12 dependent Kubernetes resource types; a secondary `NotebookConfigMapReconciler` watches FeatureStore CRs and ConfigMaps to inject Feast client configuration into Kubeflow Notebook workbenches. The serving stack exposes both HTTP and gRPC interfaces: a Go feature server handles online feature retrieval (`/get-online-features`, `/health`) with Prometheus metrics, while a Python FastAPI/Starlette server exposes a comprehensive REST API for registry management, feature serving, monitoring, lineage tracking, and a UI. The Python SDK also provides gRPC services for registry, serving, transformation, and health checking. Authentication is CRD-configurable: operators select exactly one of Kubernetes RBAC or OIDC authorization, enforced by the Python permissions framework (`SecurityManager`, `AuthManager`), with TLS optionally configured via Secret references or OpenShift service serving certificates. [source: infra/feast-operator/api/v1alpha1/featurestore_types.go:611-634, 636-657, 706, infra/feast-operator/internal/controller/featurestore_controller.go:366-376, sdk/python/feast/permissions/server/utils.py:45-52]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nFeast follows a two-tier architecture pattern: a cluster-scoped Go operator manages the Kubernetes lifecycle of feature store deployments, while the actual feature serving is performed by separate workloads deployed as Deployments under the operator's ownership. The operator uses controller-runtime v0.23.3 with two reconcilers. The primary `FeatureStoreReconciler` watches the `FeatureStore` CR and owns 12 Kubernetes resource types including Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, ServiceAccounts, Roles/RoleBindings, HorizontalPodAutoscalers, PodDisruptionBudgets, CronJobs, PersistentVolumeClaims, and OpenShift Routes. This broad ownership surface reflects the operator's role as a comprehensive lifecycle manager rather than a simple deployment controller. The secondary `NotebookConfigMapReconciler` injects Feast client configuration into Kubeflow Notebook workbenches, creating an integration point with the notebook platform.\n\nThe feature serving stack is implemented in both Go and Python. The Go feature server provides a minimal, high-performance online serving path with `/get-online-features` and `/health` HTTP endpoints, plus gRPC `ServingService` and `Health` services. It connects to pluggable online stores (PostgreSQL via pgx, Redis/Valkey via go-redis) and registries (S3, GCS). The Python stack is significantly broader: a FastAPI/Starlette application serves over 90 REST endpoints covering registry CRUD, feature serving, monitoring, lineage tracking (including OpenLineage integration), active learning, and a web UI. Python gRPC services provide parallel access for `RegistryServer` (55+ RPCs), `GrpcFeatureServer`, and `TransformationService`.\n\nThe authentication model is noteworthy for its configurability and its security implications. The `AuthzConfig` CRD field enforces exactly one of Kubernetes RBAC or OIDC authorization via a kubebuilder validation rule. The Python permissions framework (`SecurityManager` → `AuthManager`) supports three runtime modes: `no_auth` (AllowAll), `oidc` (OIDC token parsing via PyJWT), and `kubernetes` (Kubernetes token validation). However, the Go feature server's gRPC and HTTP handler chains have no authentication interceptors or middleware — they rely on network-level isolation or external enforcement. The operator's own metrics endpoint is secured via controller-runtime's TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview filter with self-signed TLS. Health and readiness probes are intentionally unauthenticated.\n\nThe operator manages RBAC extensively: the `feast-operator-manager-role` ClusterRole grants 23 rules spanning core resources, CRDs (feast.dev, kubeflow.org, monitoring.coreos.com, sparkoperator.k8s.io), OpenShift APIs (routes, apiservers), and RBAC management (ClusterRoles, ClusterRoleBindings, Roles, RoleBindings). This broad RBAC surface enables the operator to create per-namespace authorization structures when Kubernetes RBAC auth is selected. External integrations include online stores (PostgreSQL, Redis/Valkey), object storage registries (S3, GCS), compute engines (Ray, Kubernetes Jobs, Spark), and monitoring (prometheus-operator ServiceMonitors). [source: infra/feast-operator/internal/controller/featurestore_controller.go:366-376, infra/feast-operator/api/v1alpha1/featurestore_types.go:611-634, sdk/python/feast/permissions/server/utils.py:45-63, sdk/python/feast/permissions/auth/auth_manager.py:60-68, go/internal/feast/server/http_server.go:388-389, infra/feast-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:2]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage\n\n| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Upstream | https://github.com/feast-dev/feast | auto_merge | stable | -- | sync_config |\n| Midstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/feast | auto_merge | stable | -- | sync_config |\n| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/feast | auto_merge | stable | `sync_downstream_branch.yml`, `sync_stable_branch.yml`, `sync_upstream.yml` | local_analysis |\n\n### Aliases\n\n| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n\n## Architecture Components\n\n| Component | Type | Purpose |\n|---------|----|-------|\n| Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.feast-operator.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| cmd | Go controller-runtime operator | cmd |\n| feast | Go executable, Python SDK, Python console script | Executable Go command selected by the runtime image build; Python SDK for Feast; feast.cli.cli:cli |\n| feast-operator-controller-manager | Deployment | manager (controller:latest) |\n| go | Go executable | go |\n| go/infra/docker/feature-server/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"./feast\"] |\n| gunicorn | Python ASGI/WSGI server | Python entrypoint |\n| infra/feast-operator/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| java/infra/docker/feature-server/Dockerfile.dev:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"java\",\\ |\n| java/infra/docker/feature-server/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"java\",\\ |\n| java/serving/src/test/resources/docker-compose/feast10/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"./entrypoint.sh\"] |\n| manager | Go executable | Executable Go command selected by the runtime image build |\n| sdk/python/feast/infra/compute_engines/aws_lambda/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [ \"app.handler\" ] |\n| sdk/python/feast/infra/transformation_servers/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [ \"python\", \"app.py\" ] |\n----\n# Component: feast-module-operator\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/feast-module-operator.git\n- **Version**: 35188e9fc1df0ba279e2ec20410bd27d890f7758\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Go\n- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages Feast feature store deployments on OpenShift as a module within the RHOAI platform, reconciling the FeastOperator custom resource to render, deploy, and lifecycle-manage Feast components via the opendatahub-operator reconciler framework. [source: cmd/main.go:29, internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_controller.go:88, api/components/v1alpha1/feastoperator_types.go:20]\n\n**Detailed**: feast-module-operator is a Go-based Kubernetes operator built on controller-runtime and the opendatahub-operator reconciler framework. It watches the cluster-scoped `FeastOperator` custom resource (components.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1) and reconciles Feast feature store infrastructure by rendering kustomize manifests and deploying them into the applications namespace. The operator manages a broad set of child resources including Deployments, Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC resources, Jobs, CronJobs, ConfigMaps, ServiceMonitors, and PodDisruptionBudgets. It implements a platform version handshake protocol via the `odh-feastoperator-config` ConfigMap, enabling coordinated upgrades with the RHOAI platform operator. The operator also watches Feast FeatureStore CRDs (feast.dev), Kubeflow Notebooks, OpenShift Routes, and Spark applications as part of its integration surface. [source: internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_controller.go:88-135, internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_platform_version.go:50-93, pkg/manager/manager.go:66-151]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nfeast-module-operator follows the ODH module operator pattern: a Cobra CLI entrypoint dispatches to an `operator` subcommand (with a secondary `chartgen` subcommand for offline manifest generation). The operator subcommand constructs a controller-runtime manager with namespace-scoped caching limited to the applications namespace, then registers a single reconciler for the FeastOperator CR. [source: cmd/main.go:31-43, cmd/operator/operator.go:40-54, pkg/manager/manager.go:86-111]\n\nThe reconciler is built using the opendatahub-operator's declarative action pipeline: `initialize → upgradeIfNeeded → setKustomizedParams → releases → reconcilePlatformVersion → kustomize render → migrateDeploymentSelector → deploy → deployments status → gc`. This pipeline renders kustomize manifests from a bundled manifests directory, applies them with ordering and caching, and garbage-collects stale resources. A finalizer cleans up cluster-scoped ClusterRoles and ClusterRoleBindings that cannot use owner references. [source: internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_controller.go:88-143, internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_controller.go:148-178]\n\nA notable architectural feature is the platform version handshake: the operator watches a ConfigMap (`odh-feastoperator-config`) in the applications namespace and compares its `platformVersion` value against the version recorded in the FeastOperator status. When the platform version advances, the operator runs version-gated migration logic before updating the status record. This enables the RHOAI platform operator to coordinate upgrades across module boundaries. [source: internal/controller/feastoperator/feastoperator_platform_version.go:50-93]\n\nThe operator's RBAC footprint is broad, reflecting its responsibility for deploying the full Feast stack: it manages Deployments, Services, RBAC resources, Jobs, CronJobs, PVCs, HPAs, PDBs, and Spark applications. It also requires read access to Kubeflow Notebooks and OpenShift Routes, suggesting integration with workbench and dashboard components. The metrics endpoint uses controller-runtime's built-in authentication via TokenReview and SubjectAccessReview, with a dedicated metrics-auth ClusterRole. [source: config/rbac/role.yaml:2, config/rbac/metrics_auth_role.yaml:1-17, pkg/manager/manager.go:91-93]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98b7402301a08c80","agent_id":"a1cc435330cc27217","session_id":"ca7cb4a8-97e2-442a-8df6-df0a1f4664f3","uuid":"ea26feea-f121-4863-96c2-1f12990ec5ee","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:09:54.120Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-95f3da54f8cdeb2b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8007"}
